  "payload": {
    "rawResponseText": "Converting an **independent hotel to a franchise** means affiliating the property with a hotel brand so you can operate under that brand’s standards, systems, and distribution network in exchange for fees and required upgrades.[2][3] The process typically starts with evaluating the hotel’s physical condition, market position, and competitive set, then selecting a brand, reviewing the franchise agreement, and completing any property improvement plan (PIP) before reopening under the new flag.[1][2]\n\nKey steps usually include:\n\n- **Assess the asset and market fit**: review condition, room mix, public spaces, technology, and performance versus competitors.[1]\n- **Choose the right brand**: match the hotel’s location, target guest, and positioning to a brand tier that fits the market.[1][4]\n- **Model the costs**: account for initial franchise fees, ongoing royalty/marketing/loyalty fees, technology upgrades, renovation capex, and transition costs.[1][3][8]\n- **Negotiate and sign the franchise agreement**: franchisors may review feasibility, territory, and operating terms before approval.[1][2]\n- **Complete the PIP and conversion work**: update guestrooms, public areas, systems, signage, and brand standards as required.[1][2][4]\n- **Train and relaunch**: align operations, distribution, revenue strategy, and staff training with the new brand.[1][2][4]\n\nTypical fee structure includes an **initial franchise fee** plus ongoing **royalties** and marketing or reservation-system contributions; one source says these recurring fees often total roughly **8–12% of revenue** in many cases.[3] Conversion costs vary widely by brand and property condition; for example, one Wyndham-related source gives conversion investment ranges that can differ substantially from new-build costs, and a legal hospitality guide cites brand-specific application and initial fee examples for Wyndham and Marriott.[5]\n\nIf you want, I can also turn this into a **step-by-step conversion checklist**, a **cost estimate template**, or a **brand-selection comparison**.",
